Teach LLVM to read Xbox 360 PPC COFF (IMAGE_FILE_MACHINE_POWERPCBE = 0x01F2)

Applies to: llvm/llvm-project tag llvmorg-22.1.8
            commit ca7933e47d3a3451d81e72ac174dcb5aa28b59d1

    git clone --depth 1 --branch llvmorg-22.1.8 https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project
    cd llvm-project && git apply /path/to/ppcbe.patch

Five changes, all additive; nothing existing is modified.

  1. COFF.h            IMAGE_FILE_MACHINE_POWERPCBE = 0x1F2, and enum
                       RelocationTypesPPC (LLVM has never had one).
  2. Magic.cpp         identify_magic() gains `case 0xF2`, falling through to
                       the same `Magic[1] == 0x01` test 0xF0 already uses. This
                       is the ONLY change needed to make the file parse at all.
  3. WindowsMachineFlag.h  map the machine to Triple::ppc, so llvm-objdump
                       picks a BIG-endian PowerPC disassembler without -triple
                       and COFFObjectFile::getRelocationTypeName() reaches the
                       new arm. COFF structure fields stay little-endian --
                       LLVM reads every one through a hardcoded ulittle*_t, so
                       nothing byte-swaps as a consequence of this.
  4. COFFObjectFile.cpp  reloc type names for Triple::ppc, and "COFF-PowerPC-BE"
                       as the format name.
  5. COFFDumper.cpp    print the machine's name rather than a bare 0x1F2.

WHERE THE RELOCATION NAMES COME FROM, and what they are not.  LLVM has no PPC
COFF relocation table of its own, so the enum added in (1) is transcribed from
the MS PE/COFF specification revision 6.0 (Feb 1999) sec. 5.2.6, cross-checked
against gimli-rs/object src/pe.rs. They are therefore NOT independent evidence:
a cross-check that relies on these names is checking two written sources against
each other. What IS independent is everything above the name -- the record
decode, the offsets, the symbol indices -- because that code is LLVM's and
predates this patch.

Not attempted: writing PPC COFF (LLVM has no PPCWinCOFFObjectWriter and no
.pdata/.xdata support for this ABI), VMX128 decoding, and CodeView register
names, which come out as x86 register names for a PPC compilation unit because
the CodeView register enum is machine-dependent and LLVM only carries the x86,
ARM and ARM64 tables.

